confirmed.evidence:
isDue includes background unconditionally:
```swift
private static func isDue(_ task: [String: Any], now: Int) -> Bool {
  let due = (task["nextRunAt"] as? Int).map { $0 <= now } ?? false
  let isBackground = (task["triggers"] as? [[String: Any]])?
    .contains { ($0["type"] as? String) == "background" } ?? false
  return due || isBackground
}
```
and dispatch(manual:false) fires with no due check (only policy + budget):
```swift
if !manual {
  let decision = PolicyEngine.evaluate(TaskMapper.constraints(task), DeviceInfo.read(now: now))
  if !decision.eligible { ...; return false }
  if !tryAdmit(id, task, now: now) { ...; return false }
}
...
emit("onTaskExecute", [...])
...
advanceRecurrence(task)
return true
```
Kotlin documents and enforces exactly this gate (ExpoConductorModule.kt 211-217):
```kotlin
val nextRunAt = if (task.isNull("nextRunAt")) null else task.optLong("nextRunAt")
if (nextRunAt != null && System.currentTimeMillis() < nextRunAt) {
  return false
}
```
and TS runDueTasksAsync filters `t.nextRunAt != null && t.nextRunAt <= now` (WebSchedulerEngine.ts 161), so a future-dated background+recurrence task is never in the due set.

TS guards paused (WebSchedulerEngine.ts 155-156):
```ts
async runDueTasksAsync(): Promise<number> {
  if (this.paused) return 0;
```
Swift runDueBackgroundTasks has no paused check:
```swift
func runDueBackgroundTasks() -> Int {
  let now = nowMs()
  let due = store.all().filter { Self.isDue($0, now: now) }
  var fired = 0
  for task in due where dispatch(task, manual: false) { fired += 1 }
  return fired
}
```
and its dispatch(manual:false) checks only policy + budget, never paused. Kotlin runDueTasksAsync is the same (ExpoConductorModule.kt 77-86): it computes `due` and does `due.count { dispatch(it, manual = false) }` with no `if (paused)` guard, and dispatch has none either. Note the headless paths DO check paused (Kotlin dispatchHeadless line 345 `if (TaskStore(context).isPaused()) return`; Swift runDueBackgroundTasksHeadless line 175 `if store.isPaused() { return }`), so the live path omission is inconsistent even within each native module.

Kotlin counts and runs the handler in the same synchronous loop:
```kotlin
// runDueTasksAsync
due.count { dispatch(it, manual = false) }
```
```kotlin
// dispatch, native branch
if (handlerType == "native") {
  val handler = nativeHandlers[handlerName]
  val result = handler?.run(id, data) ?: "noData"
  running.remove(id)
  emitComplete(id, result)
}
```
Kotlin handler type (ConductorTaskHandler.kt 9-16) is `fun interface ... fun run(taskId: String, data: Map<String, Any?>): String` — no throws guarantee. Swift handler type (ConductorTaskHandler.swift 9) is `public typealias ConductorTaskHandler = (_ taskId: String, _ data: [String: Any]) -> String` — non-throwing, so dispatch's `ConductorHandlerRegistry.shared.handler(for: handlerName)?(id, data) ?? "noData"` cannot throw.

Swift (only the policy/budget block is under `if !manual`, then the advance is unconditional):
```
    if !manual {
      let decision = PolicyEngine.evaluate(...)
      ...
      if !tryAdmit(id, task, now: now) { ... }
    }
    ...
    advanceRecurrence(task)
    return true
```
Web gates it: `if (!manual) this.reschedule(task, now);` (WebSchedulerEngine.ts line 354). Kotlin gates it with the explicit parity comment: `// A manual run-now must not advance the task's real schedule (matches the TS engine).` then `if (!manual) advanceRecurrence(task)` (ExpoConductorModule.kt lines 272-273).

Swift bails out before any notification/re-arm work:
```
  static func dispatchHeadless(_ task: [String: Any], data: [String: Any]) {
    guard let id = task["id"] as? String,
          let handler = task["handler"] as? [String: Any],
          (handler["type"] as? String) == "native" else { return }
```
Android handles the JS+notification case (ExpoConductorModule.kt 354-373): it shows the notification (`NotificationDisplay.show(...)`) and only returns early when `!isNative && notif == null`, then re-arms: `if (!isNative && notif == null) return; val recurrence = ... ; task.put("nextRunAt", next); TaskStore(context).upsert(task); ...`.
